* Every prime below a hundred, by trying every divisor.
*
* The division is the snippet from the esolangs catalogue, pasted into
* `lib/fast.bfm` whole -- a pointer walking a fixed workspace, which is what
* the macro language cannot express and why it is pasted rather than written.
* The loop below invokes it like anything else and never sees it.
*
* That it can be invoked *from inside a loop* is what this program is really
* demonstrating. A scan makes the emitted movement stop meaning anything, so
* until the `]` learned to measure a body by where it began and ended rather
* than by what it emitted, an idiom like this one could only appear in a
* straight line -- which is no use, because a division belongs in a loop.
*
* The whole program:
*
*     for n in range(2, 100):
*         if all(n % d for d in range(2, n)):
*             print(n, end=' ')
*     print()
*
* Answer: twenty-five numbers, and a fact about arithmetic rather than about
* this program.

@include "lib/fast.bfm"

@var n            * the candidate
@var d            * the divisor being tried
@var q
@var r
@var ten
@var prime        * whether nothing has divided the candidate yet
@var trying       * whether there are divisors left to try
@var tens
@var going
@var flag
@var count
@var step
@var passed
@var other
@var scratch
@var spare
@var out

* A number below a hundred, without a leading zero. The same `@divmod`, given
* ten: a digit is a remainder.
*
*     tens, r = divmod(n, 10)
*     if tens:
*         print(tens, end='')
*     print(r, end='')
@macro show_number {
    @divmod(n, ten, tens, r)
    @is_not_zero(tens, flag, scratch, spare)
    @to flag
    [
        @show_digit(tens)
        @clear(flag)
    ]
    @show_digit(r)
}

@put(ten, 10)
@put(n, 2)
@put(going, 1)
@to going
[
    @put(prime, 1)
    @put(d, 2)

    * Every divisor below the candidate. Stopping at its square root would
    * save half the work and cost a comparison; a hundred candidates is not
    * enough work to buy one.
    @less(d, n, trying, count, step, passed, flag, other, scratch, spare)
    @to trying
    [
        @divmod(n, d, q, r)
        @is_zero(r, flag, scratch, spare)
        @to flag
        [
            @clear(prime)
            @clear(flag)
        ]
        @to d
        +
        @less(d, n, trying, count, step, passed, flag, other, scratch, spare)
        @to trying
    ]

    @to prime
    [
        @show_number
        @say(out, SPACE)
        @clear(prime)
    ]

    @to n
    +
    @put(count, 100)
    @equal(count, n, scratch, spare)
    @to count
    [
        @clear(going)
        @clear(count)
    ]
    @to going
]
@newline(out)
